%N A401594 Square array read by antidiagonals upwards: T(n,k) is the n-th prime that does not contain the string "k", with n >= 1, k >= 0.
%C A401594 Numbers in the column k could be called k-free primes. In that case T(n,k) could be called the n-th k-free prime.
%H A401594 Paolo Xausa, <a href="/A401594/b401594.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..11325</a> (first 150 antidiagonals, flattened).
%H A401594 James Maynard, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1604.01041">Primes with restricted digits</a>, arXiv:1604.01041 [math.NT], 2016-2019.
%H A401594 James Maynard and Brady Haran, <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eeoBCS7IEqs">Primes without a 7</a>, Numberphile YouTube video, 2019.
%e A401594 The corner 13 X 13 of the square array is as shown below:
